1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Here's something worth getting straight.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Clod is one model,

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:06,000
but you reach it through very different surfaces,

4
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
and they don't all behave the same way.

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
The raw API, the agent SDK,

6
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
and Clod code each wrap that model differently.

7
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
This lesson maps what stays the same

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
and what shifts between those options.

9
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Let's break it down. Let's name

10
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
the three main ways you reach Clod in code,

11
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
because each one hands you a different starting point.

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
The first is the raw API.

13
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
You call the messages endpoint directly and supply

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
every instruction yourself through the system parameter.

15
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Nothing is auto-loaded for you.

16
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The second is the agent SDK.

17
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:43,000
It runs the agent loop on your behalf,

18
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,000
but its default system prompt is deliberately minimal,

19
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:48,000
just enough for tool calling,

20
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
not the full Clod code prompt.

21
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The third is Clod code itself,

22
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
the command line coding agent.

23
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,000
It loads a full system prompt,

24
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
the entire Clod.md hierarchy,

25
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:03,000
and auto-memory the moment a session starts.

26
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Same model underneath,

27
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:07,000
three very different amounts of scaffolding.

28
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Let's build the right mental model.

29
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Underneath every surface,

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
the model's core behavior is constant.

31
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Given the same instructions and the same context,

32
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
it reasons the same way.

33
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
What changes from one surface to the next is the wrapper around it.

34
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Tool access is one axis.

35
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
It can range from no tools at all on a bare API call-up,

36
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
to a full tool set in Clod code.

37
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:35,000
File and config loading is another.

38
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Some surfaces read files and configuration off-disk,

39
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,000
others touch nothing you didn't hand them.

40
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
The session and memory model varies too.

41
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,000
When a behavior looks different across surfaces,

42
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,000
the first suspect is the scaffolding, not the model.

43
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Now let's pin down where your instructions actually live,

44
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,000
because it's different on every surface. On the raw API,

45
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
instructions go in the system parameter you set on the request,

46
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
and nothing else is loaded for you.

47
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
On the agent SDK,

48
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
you get a minimal default prompt,

49
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:11,000
plus your Clod.md when the matching setting sources are enabled,

50
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:12,000
which they are by default.

51
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
In Clod code, a full system prompt loads along with

52
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
the whole Clod.md hierarchy, your rules,

53
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:22,000
and auto-memory, all at launch.

54
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:23,000
On a hosted chat surface,

55
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,000
your profile and project custom instructions apply,

56
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:29,000
but only inside that web surface.

57
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Same instruction, four different homes.

58
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:34,000
Let's zoom in on the raw API,

59
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:36,000
because it's the most bare-bones surface,

60
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
and that makes the pattern obvious.

61
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,000
There's no magic here.

62
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
You get exactly what you configure and not one thing more.

63
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Your only channel for system level instruction is

64
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
the system parameter on the request, that's it.

65
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,000
There's no Clod.md being read off-disk,

66
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,000
no presets, and no auto-memory carrying anything between calls.

67
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Persona, safety rules, and tool guidance

68
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,000
are all yours to write and send every time.

69
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
The upside is total control over what the model sees.

70
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:10,000
The trade-off is total responsibility.

71
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,000
If you didn't put an instruction in the request,

72
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
the model simply never got it.

73
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,000
Here's a distinction that trips people up constantly.

74
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:20,000
So let's be precise.

75
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,000
When you use the agent SDK,

76
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:25,000
its default system prompt is minimal.

77
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
It's built for tool calling and little else.

78
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,000
It isn't the Clod code prompt.

79
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
If you want the SDK to behave like the command line agent,

80
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
you set the Clod code preset explicitly,

81
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
and you can append your own text on top.

82
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Clod code itself is the opposite default.

83
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
Even when you run it headless in print mode,

84
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
it uses the full Clod code system prompt

85
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and loads its whole config stack

86
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
without you opting into anything.

87
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
So the same underlying loop feels very different

88
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
depending on which default you inherited.

89
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Name the surface before you assume the prompt.

90
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
This is the single most misunderstood piece,

91
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
so let's slow down.

92
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
The SDK doesn't load your Clod.md

93
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:12,000
because you turned on the Clod code preset.

94
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Those are two separate switches.

95
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
What actually controls Clod.md loading is setting sources.

96
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:24,000
The project source loads the Clod.md in your repository.

97
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:29,000
The user source loads the one in your home config directory.

98
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Here's the reassuring part.

99
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:34,000
The default query options enable both of those sources,

100
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
so out of the box, your Clod.md does load automatically.

101
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
But the moment you pass an explicit sources list, you own it.

102
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
Pass an empty list and no Clod.md loads at all, preset or not.

103
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
Setting sources decide every time.

104
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Let's cover the hosted chat surfaces,

105
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
the ones you open in a browser or an app

106
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:58,000
rather than call from code.

107
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
On Clod.ai, you can set profile custom instructions

108
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
and project instructions,

109
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
and every conversation in that project inherits them.

110
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
Clod Desktop is a comparable consumer chat app

111
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:15,000
with its own custom instructions and connectors.

112
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The exact precedence mechanics there are lighter,

113
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:19,000
so we'll keep it high level.

114
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,000
Here's the point that matters for a builder.

115
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Everything you set in these chat apps is surface scoped.

116
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Those instructions never reach your Clod code sessions

117
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
or your raw API calls.

118
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
Each surface keeps its own configuration,

119
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
so a rule you typed in the web app

120
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:38,000
won't follow the model into your code.

121
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Let's turn this into a debugging habit

122
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,000
because you will hit a behavior difference

123
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
between surfaces sooner or later.

124
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
The instinct is to blame the model or the prompt. Resist that.

125
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:54,000
First, separate the interface layer from the model output.

126
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Reproduce the exact same prompt on a second surface,

127
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:00,000
say the raw API, where you control everything.

128
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,000
Then check what each surface actually loaded,

129
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:07,000
its config, its system prompt, and its tools,

130
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:10,000
because that's usually where the difference lives.

131
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Only after you've ruled out the scaffolding

132
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
should you look at the model output itself.

133
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Nine times out of 10, the surprise traces back

134
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
to a loaded config or a missing tool,

135
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
not to the model reasoning differently. Let's recap.

136
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
One Clod model reaches you through several surfaces

137
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
and each wraps it with different scaffolding.

138
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The raw API gives you only the system parameter,

139
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:39,000
the agent SDK defaults to a minimal prompt.

140
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Clod code loads the full prompt and hierarchy.

141
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Keep in mind, setting sources, not the preset,

142
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:48,000
are what load clod.md.

143
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:50,000
That covers how one model behaves

144
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
across many surfaces and SDKs.

145
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:54,000
I'll see you in the next one.

